{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And you shall make the altar of shittim wood.</b> This is the altar of burnt offering, and it was placed in the court of the Tabernacle, and upon it the sacrifices were offered, and it was overlaid with copper. And it was square, corresponding to the fourth leg in the Chariot, which is the fourth letter of the Name, and concerning this it says: ״רבוע יהיה המזבח״ — 'the altar shall be square.'" "<b>And know</b> that the height of this altar was ten cubits, and what it says, ״ושלש אמות קומתו״ — 'and three cubits its height' — was said only regarding the place of the arrangement of wood. And the proof that its height was ten cubits is from what our Rabbis of blessed memory said: ״רבוע יהיה״ — for what does it come? It is available (מופנה), for since it teaches us ״five cubits long and five cubits wide,״ I already know that it is square; and what do I learn from ״רבוע יהיה״? Rather, it comes for a gezerah shavah: it says here ״רבוע יהיה״ and it says regarding the incense altar ״רבוע יהיה״, as it is written (Exodus 30:1): ״ועשית מזבח מקטר קטורת אמה ארכו ואמה רחבו רבוע יהיה ואמתים קומתו״ — just as the incense altar's height is twice its width, so too here its height is twice its width. If so, how do I fulfill ״ושלש אמות קומתו״? From the edge of the surround and upward. And a further proof is from what our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded in tractate Sukkah, chapter 'Lulav and Aravah': the aravah-branches of the aravah they would place at the sides of the altar, and their tops were bent over the altar; a tanna taught: they were long and tall, eleven cubits, so that they would lean over the altar one cubit — and this is clear that the height of the altar was ten cubits. And further, our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded in tractate Zevahim: the altar that Moses made was ten cubits, for Scripture juxtaposed it to the Tabernacle, as it says (Numbers 4:26): ״ואת קלעי החצר ואת מסך פתח שער החצר אשר על המשכן ועל המזבח״ — just as the Tabernacle was ten cubits, so too the altar was ten cubits. And furthermore, there is a full verse regarding the altar that Solomon made, whose height was ten cubits, as it is written in Chronicles (II Chronicles 4:1): ״ויעש מזבח נחשת עשרים אמה ארכו ועשרים אמה רחבו ועשר אמות קומתו״, and likewise the altar of the future." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״ועשית את המזבח עצי שטים״ — the copper altar atones for the brazen forehead, as it says (Isaiah 48:4): ״וגיד ברזל ערפך ומצחך נחושה״. מזב\"ח — מ'חילה ז'כות ב'רכה ח'יים; and likewise שטי\"ם — ש'לום ט'ובה י'שועה מ'חילה. ״Five cubits long and five cubits wide״ correspond to the two tablets of the covenant — five commandments on one side and five commandments on the other; ״and three cubits its height״ corresponds to the three redeemers: Moses, Aaron, and Miriam."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:04:58.898609584Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}